WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.504
and comedian as well as
the creator and star

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.639
of the critically acclaimed
series, "Ramy."

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.941
The entire third season
is streaming now on Hulu.

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:11.377
Please welcome back to the show
Ramy Youssef, everyone.

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:13.379
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:23.389 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:27.160 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.062
Welcome back, Ramy.
-Dude.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.930
-I am so happy
to have you here in person.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.532
-I know.
In person's amazing.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.001
-You were, I guess, here
maybe three, four years ago

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.536
when the first season
of your show was out.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.404
-Yeah.
-And somehow you've managed to,

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.239
in a very hard time
to make television,

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.241
made two more seasons.
Congratulations.

00:00:42.241 --> 00:00:44.410
-Thank you.
Yeah, it was --

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.545
You guys did it, too.
We did one of these on Zoom...

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:49.482
-Yep.
-...which was also great.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:50.716
But, yeah,
seeing you in person is like --

00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:52.351
-It's a lot nicer.
-I know. I hugged you,

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.553
and I was like, I remembered
the time we all had hope.

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:56.322
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.591
-There used to be so much hope.
-Yeah.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.393
-I've been, like --
You know, I was telling you,

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.695
too, back there,
I've been thinking about

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.732
just how much I can't trust my
feelings these last few years.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:09.835
-How's that?
-Like, you know, I don't think

00:01:09.835 --> 00:01:13.406
I trust what's going on
in my head anymore.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.441
And even looking out right now,
and it's like some people

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:18.144
are wearing masks, some aren't,
and it scares me.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:21.581
What do they know that...?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.249
-Yeah.
-'Cause I'm starting to think,

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.184
you know, I'm at that point now
where I see someone with a mask,

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:27.419
and, you know...
-It's interesting.

00:01:27.419 --> 00:01:29.555
I feel like people broke
two different ways,

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:31.457
and I'm very much like you.
I feel like some people

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:34.860
are like, "I know exactly where
the pandemic's at, at all times,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.095
whatever I think is right."
-Yes.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:37.864
-Whereas I'm
constantly thinking,

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.531
"I might be doing this wrong."
-Yeah.

00:01:39.531 --> 00:01:41.934
[ Laughter ]
Well, I realized

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.002
that I can't trust myself

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.437
'cause I remember
the first mask I saw.

00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:46.806
Remember the first one?
-Yep.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:49.208
-Like, early.
I remember that guy,

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.510
just seeing him at
the supermarket, I'm like,

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.179
"Oh, look at this guy."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.315
It was like, "Okay, buddy.
Someone's been on YouTube."

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.815
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.815 --> 00:02:00.119
"You're gonna go back to your
bunker, like, what's going on."

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.854
And then, two weeks later...

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:05.825
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.994
I saw a guy without a mask,
and I was like,

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.163
"Whoa.
Look who hates Black people."

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:13.599
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:18.738
"Like, this guy does not care
about the country."

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:21.341
Immediately, I was like, "Oh,
that happened in two weeks."

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.609
-Yeah.
-Everything changed.

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:24.910
I don't trust myself anymore.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.945
-Yeah.
Rarely do you go from

00:02:26.945 --> 00:02:28.948
the first part to the second
part in just two weeks.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:29.982
-Two weeks.

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:31.483
-Usually takes years
to get there.

00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:34.921
-Oh, man, I'm just a new person,
immediately.

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.789
-One of my favorite things about
your show is, it's very funny,

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.425
but there's also a really great
narrative thrust to the show.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.894
And with that in mind, I don't
want to give anything away.

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:44.897
-Yeah.
-Because the end of season 2

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:47.266
sort of left Ramy --
And he was just thriving.

00:02:47.266 --> 00:02:50.570
Just doing great.
And then there's --

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:52.404
I will only say,
there's a bit of a time jump.

00:02:52.404 --> 00:02:54.407
Is it --
What would you tell people

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:56.975
before they go into season 3?

00:02:56.975 --> 00:02:59.679
-You know, I think the show --
the most interesting part

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:02.181
about it, to me, has always been
just this idea

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:06.085
of trying to be with someone
who wants a spiritual life.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:08.053
I think it's something that
a lot of people can relate to,

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:11.957
and it's not something
that we see in a serious way.

00:03:11.957 --> 00:03:13.426
You know, it's obviously
a comedy, so we're finding

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.861
the funny that's in it,
but just the serious pursuit.

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:19.632
And I think he's always in this
battle between his higher self

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.968
and his lower self,
but we really kind of go into

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:23.469
this one where he's like,
"You know what?

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.237
Maybe I just am my lower self,"

00:03:25.237 --> 00:03:26.673
and he kind of believes that
for a while.

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:28.974
And so we're seeing
this crisis of faith.

00:03:28.974 --> 00:03:32.244
-There's a very funny scene
about Christmas,

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:34.313
and this is based on your own
personal history.

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:36.249
But can we show the clip first
and then discuss?

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:38.050
-Yeah.
-Let's take a look.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.852
-Our traditions
have a lot in common.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.386
-Yeah, I've always felt that.

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:48.227
That we share this deep
Christmaslessness, you know?

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:49.328
-Huh?

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:51.564
-Like not celebrating Christmas,
right?

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:53.499
Like, the whole country --
this whole country's, like,

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:56.035
worshipping Santa,
and we're like,

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.837
"No, I don't think something --
This doesn't feel right."

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:58.971
You know?
-Hmm.

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:00.672
-I remember being
in kindergarten,

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:02.108
everyone's talking about Santa.

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:03.610
I look over at this kid, Ari,

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.479
and I'm like,
"Dude, we know the truth.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:09.449
We know this is just,
like, a capitalist [bleep] lie."

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.017
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.719
-Christmaslessness,
was this something that you

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.522
found common cause with other
kids in school growing up?

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:18.324
-Oh, this was deep.
Me and my Jewish friends,

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.293
we were the only ones
who knew the truth.

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:22.294
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:24.830
-Did you keep it to yourself?
-Well, you try,

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.999
but then you say something,
and then you get in trouble.

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:28.467
And then they're like,
"What are you doing?"

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:32.305
"I'm telling the truth."
And it's so wild, like,

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.474
this feeling of being a kid

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:37.109
and realizing you're just
surrounded by lies.

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:40.012
Like, I remember watching,
even TV, I saw a clip as a child

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:42.848
of Bill Clinton, and he's on TV,
and he's like,

00:04:42.848 --> 00:04:44.383
"Santa's coming to your homes.

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:45.651
I hope he brings you
nice gifts."

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:47.486
And I was like, in that moment,
I was like,

00:04:47.486 --> 00:04:49.222
"I will never trust
this government."

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:51.224
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:53.459
Ever. I mean, that's the guy
we're supposed to trust?

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.595
I'm supposed to believe these
people about Guantanamo?

00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:58.498
Like, this is --
it's dark, you know?

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:00.533
And you really --
Yeah, you're a child,

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:04.570
and you know about the Christmas
industrial complex.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.605
[ Laughter ]
It's damaging.

00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:09.675
-Is it fun in the writers' room
when you realize that you can --

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.777
I mean, again,
so much of the show, I imagine,

00:05:11.777 --> 00:05:13.245
is pulled
from personal experiences,

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:14.814
but it must be so great to know
that you can write

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:17.116
a Christmaslessness monologue

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:19.118
and get to perform it
as an adult.

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:22.121
Just must be the best.
-No, it's cathartic in that way.

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.089
I mean, and a lot of it, like,
I'll find on stage

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:25.691
and then bring it in,
and then that's where

00:05:25.691 --> 00:05:27.259
it's really fun kind of
having a group of people

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:29.028
who've all experienced it.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.431
-There's a great -- obviously,
a great element of the show,

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:33.165
are the people
who play your parents.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:35.100
You have parents
in real life, as well.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.804
How do they feel about the show?
What is their feedback?

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.773
-You know, they, um --

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.608
You know, they don't --
they don't like the sex scenes,

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.343
which is, like --
-Fair.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:45.978
-Yeah.
And it's your parents.

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:48.614
They don't want to see --
I tell them it's a stunt guy.

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:51.284
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.152
I tried to tell -- I'm like,
even with the sex stuff,

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:56.555
I'm like, "Look, I promise,
my next role, no sex,

00:05:56.555 --> 00:05:58.557
like, just violence."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:00.459
"Like, I'll just kill people."

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:01.627
And they're like,
"Oh, thank God."

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:02.795
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.530
-I will say, while it might
be disappointing

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.265
when your parents say,
"We don't like the sex scenes,"

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:08.500
the alternative would be worse,
right?

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:09.869
If they were like,
"Our favorite part..."

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:13.238
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.238 --> 00:06:15.474
-No, yeah, that'd be, like --
My dad, though,

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:18.044
will give me notes about --
like, he doesn't like that,

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:19.445
and then he'll just talk to me
about the lighting

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:20.846
for like an hour.
-Really?

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:22.482
-Yeah, he'll be like,
"You got to look at the lighting

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:23.582
in a couple of these scenes.

00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:25.250
I'd like to see progress
next season."

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.552
-Wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.421
Does he have a background
in lighting?

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:30.156
-Uh, no.
-Okay.

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:31.356
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.356 --> 00:06:32.591
He's just learning by watching?

00:06:32.591 --> 00:06:34.026
-There's no lighting
in our family.

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:36.596
Like, we're kind of
a dimly lit family, actually.

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:39.932
We try to save money
on the electric bill.

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:42.234
Most of my life was,
"Turn off that light."

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:46.572
-The weird thing is --
it is a beautifully shot show.

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.106
And it is funny that your dad

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:49.608
would find a way
to criticize that.

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:51.310
I mean --
-Well, he's a father.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.746
He just -- He wants
the lighting to get better.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:55.180
-Yeah, that's all.
-And I think that's all it is.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.849
-He's like,
"Look, the lighting's fine."

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:58.216
-[ Laughs ]
"It's great where it is,

00:06:58.216 --> 00:07:00.219
but I want to push you
to take it to the next level."

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:02.521
-I don't want you to settle on
the current lighting situation.

00:07:02.521 --> 00:07:05.324
-Yeah, exactly.
-I'm wondering, you know, again,

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:07.693
we're talking about how much,
you know,

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:09.261
your life inspires the show.

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:13.165
One decision you made early on
is, being an actor named Ramy

00:07:13.165 --> 00:07:15.601
who called the show "Ramy" and
plays a character named Ramy.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.203
Do you ever regret that?

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:19.404
[ Laughter ]

00:07:19.404 --> 00:07:20.940
-Every single day.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.442
Like, every -- every single day,
I'm like, "There's so many

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:25.478
other names
that we could have picked."

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:27.446
And it's funny,
because I grew up --

00:07:27.446 --> 00:07:29.549
Part of it was,
I wanted to have my name,

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:31.683
you know, and not just
'cause it was me,

00:07:31.683 --> 00:07:33.920
but because the industry
that came before me,

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:35.354
it was anyone
who had an Arab name,

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:37.924
they changed their name
in order to blend in.

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:38.991
-Right.
-If your name was Muhammad,

00:07:38.991 --> 00:07:40.526
you'd want to say you were Mike.

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:41.927
You know,
you'd try to find something.

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:43.595
And so I was like, "Yeah,
I want to have an Arab name."

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:45.797
But now -- yeah, now we're like
three seasons in,

00:07:45.797 --> 00:07:48.467
we can't change the name.

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:49.935
Now I'm like, "Well,
maybe I'll change my name."

00:07:49.935 --> 00:07:54.339
Like, I might, in real life,
just -- I might just go --

00:07:54.339 --> 00:07:56.475
like, maybe I'm just Robert now.
-Yeah.

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.845
So, the show's still "Ramy,"
but then you tell people,

00:07:58.845 --> 00:08:01.180
you're like, "That's not me."
-Yeah, and I made progress.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.382
I made it in Hollywood as an
Arab, but now I'm Robert.

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:05.217
[ Laughter ]
-Right.

00:08:05.217 --> 00:08:06.585
That's the thing you would prove
to everybody.

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.454
You're like, "I owned it."
-It can be done.

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:10.189
-Until I made it,
and now I want to be Robert.

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:11.957
-Now I'm Robert. Yeah, yeah.
It's been done.

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:13.592
-Well, I hope you stick
with Ramy.

00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:15.928
I think we all prefer the Ramy,

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:17.630
having a little Ramy
in our lives,

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.631
both on screen and off.

00:08:19.631 --> 00:08:22.634
It's really a fantastic --
We were talking backstage.

00:08:22.634 --> 00:08:24.303
I've watched the first
couple episodes of season 3.

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:25.837
It's great.
It's always been great.

00:08:25.837 --> 00:08:27.273
And it's so lovely to have you
here in person.

00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:28.474
-Thank you, man.
Thanks, dude.

00:08:28.474 --> 00:08:30.376
Thank you, thank you.
-Ramy Youssef, everybody.

00:08:30.376 --> 00:08:32.511
The third season of "Ramy"
is streaming now on Hulu.

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:34.046
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

